A||N||7||Will||'88 Front Wheel bearings||||1||
Z||000000||Will||09-24-2000||08:12 PM||||After poking around under the car for a while, I found that the rack bushings do indeed have a small amount of play, but most of it comes from the RH wheel bearing. With what cars do '88 Fieros share front wheel bearings? Will they interchange with early wheel bearings? How hard would early wheel bearings be to adapt?<BR>     Is zero the only acceptable amount of play in the rack bushings?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||09-24-2000||09:56 PM||||Not sure about how much play is normal for the rack bushings. <BR>Bearings shouldn't have any noticeable play at all. (Are you sure it's not the ball joints that have the play?) As far as I know, there are no other cars that share the 88 front wheel bearings. I don't think you can adapt anything else. It's very straight-forward to replace the original, though. It's a sealed hub/bearing unit. Just three big Torx bolts hold it to the steering knuckle. If it's not rusted/crudded into place (like mine was), it will take you about 15 minutes to replace. Waayyyyy easier than replacing/repacking "normal" bearings. They are <I>not</I> cheap, however. I've heard anywhere from $80 - $140 <I>per wheel</I>! Shop around for the best price. Most everyone will have to special order them. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||09-24-2000||10:37 PM||||Yes, I'm sure it's the wheel bearing. I was able to see play of the wheel relative to the steering knuckle as my father rocked the wheel up and down.<BR>     I also just replaced all four ball joints, which were worn out, too.<BR>     I know that changing them is straight forward. I would budget more than fifteen minutes, though, as the caliper must be removed from the spindle.<BR>     I want to adapt a boneyard replacement precisely because of the expense.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ozzy||09-24-2000||10:40 PM||||From memory, the Fiero front hub assy. needs replaced as a whole unit. Around $100.00 from the Fiero Store last time I checked. I don't think there is a cheap swap or else someone here would have found it by now. This is not the first time this question has been asked.<P>Ozzy||||reg||2
Z||000004||Fleabitdog||09-25-2000||09:42 PM||||I called the Fiero Store, they said they can't get them anymore. I called Delco they discontinued them but gave me a BCA# I called BCA they discontinued them, I ended up finding a used hub and bearing. I have heard if you have an arbor press you can press out the old bearing and press in the new but supposedly it is a pain. Hope you are able to locate new front bearings somewhere. If you do please let me know or post it here Thanks and good luck  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||09-25-2000||09:52 PM||||I know its kinda off the topic, but I am going to rebuild my 86GT soon and was wondering, will I have any trouble finding these parts for my car? I wont have much time to do the auctual rebuilding so I need to find sources for parts if I discover I need them. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||09-25-2000||10:25 PM||||Sean, it shouldn't be a problem for you to find bearings for your car.<P>BTW... who do you know here in Ogden? Do they own an 85GT and a white 84 with an IMSA spoiler?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||09-26-2000||04:58 PM||||No, they dont own a Fiero, they are looking into getting one after they drove mine, but they dont yet. Last name is Melany. Dennis and Linda. Live across from a school on Gentile street believe. There just family of mine.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
